#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530553} :activity/id #uuid "e08589a3-02d8-594e-8593-48db9790c753" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/id #uuid "1725b401-08f0-5bd3-9105-3249e65bebf9" :topic/math-academy-id 3211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d902eda-8b02-59d9-817f-5548cb8fa47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{2x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312196} {:answer/id #uuid "626a0064-27a3-570a-9c3a-b1e55d495db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312759} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd9d1e5-b8dd-5125-8738-bdcfe733d92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312855} {:answer/id #uuid "784800d0-17ce-5cef-9605-5d1cd6a346f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06aeca6-ffb9-5370-9b57-f3f69e5fd517" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208030f7-aa5b-5fe3-a831-ca0b389d652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312220} {:answer/id #uuid "9095fcf6-3fc8-53ea-a71e-67a00e1fa891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313128} {:answer/id #uuid "999f7576-c8ca-5d99-bba8-73458fe88f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313191} {:answer/id #uuid "ef630254-396a-5be5-81c2-167737583600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ce9ea6-e543-5afd-84eb-289c55556054" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "faaf0705-3b7e-515b-ad1e-e97ff79382c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338729" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} :step/id #uuid "216f8e14-7c94-5ef7-babd-78281fa1fe2d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80717e13-aa04-534e-b7cb-b9dd198b1171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391697} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a1003-518c-5978-82a3-b12124ff2cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391698} {:answer/id #uuid "cb175292-1d19-53ba-a268-6e1a4ae2b1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391699} {:answer/id #uuid "ab342eb6-9f44-5413-9868-ba9ddfbb18a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391700} {:answer/id #uuid "94b96273-f78c-566b-9d25-97a799b4f55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89af3df-4153-5000-b2fe-f6e14e30ad1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3af5ddc-3890-5c59-8803-f398173deb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391703} {:answer/id #uuid "0044f4b1-e6d3-557b-b79a-d102edcc257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391704} {:answer/id #uuid "4961ff2e-c297-5af0-a99e-d7db5b609a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391705} {:answer/id #uuid "40f7e995-e510-5677-a3ee-2e9610868cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391706} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c87594-7fd9-5262-b977-690b21e75561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} {:answer/id #uuid "56ed424b-8ca9-59d2-8528-1ebfe5b468f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} :answer-field/id #uuid "d664b659-e57c-53ea-8b4c-aa060927c521" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b03c659-cc6c-5259-9cfb-c53b896cc809" :question/math-academy-id "q-338909" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter value corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "636715cd-0d7e-58bd-89a4-fedf8f61d582" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "877ab039-605d-549e-8622-6cae0d465fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397254} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf99903-ee3b-5e01-a931-22d951facaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397255} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a527a7-acdb-51b7-9ade-49877be410a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397256} {:answer/id #uuid "479d00a1-2920-59e5-b016-b0576cc21641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397254} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ec000b-c994-55b9-b093-1daaa5f63bf9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50817ef9-0b55-5b6b-94aa-573ea49716ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397259} {:answer/id #uuid "42571170-ae49-58a1-a447-73aed3d500ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{20}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397260} {:answer/id #uuid "219f78d1-948d-5466-8f67-a7b5b558a098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{20}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397261} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1b1192-6a7f-53e2-8bde-b8b0685bd05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397260} :answer-field/id #uuid "75123b29-65f0-56eb-bdd5-9f239c208387" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e67017cf-eb62-5988-9350-d0872682f901" :question/math-academy-id "q-338905" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda x}+B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 10A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{20\\lambda }+2B{e}^{20\\lambda }+2(10A\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }+B{e}^{20\\lambda }+20B\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(10A\\lambda +B+20B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+20A\\lambda +2B+40B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+(4+40\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda  & 1+10\\lambda  \\\\ 1+20\\lambda  & 4(1+10\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+10\\lambda )-(1+10\\lambda )(1+20\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -(1+20\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -1-20\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )(20\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} :step/id #uuid "657eea8d-526f-550d-b00d-4e37eca243c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "567fc8bd-0545-5514-bad2-2530be47b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312673} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0f9caa-c235-5c33-97a8-ed37267ce102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312970} {:answer/id #uuid "8a976a0d-04cc-5469-b5d5-ce40485a3111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313076} {:answer/id #uuid "d4033229-d039-5591-9725-ef504b3bf910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312673} :answer-field/id #uuid "9766fe16-4357-5c95-855f-d195ff0899f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314420} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0c1d35-619b-50f3-8ba4-8bf44b8735d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312166} {:answer/id #uuid "33b15917-a630-5540-8ff2-3c7afd154552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312391} {:answer/id #uuid "dff83fa5-d810-5b3d-93f2-da5aeaca018d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313737} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a4fe9-3896-59ae-b9c8-70c510922d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312166} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6ef2dc-9acf-53d3-a7cb-4c48b67ab3c3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b03b233d-646c-56b8-aed5-c92855708d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-338921" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d86b4168-283b-56b1-8e35-bb1e41b82281"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6333f256-57a4-524f-882c-85ce13ed4d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397212} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff980e-244b-5407-8c35-36869326e1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397213} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9fce80-afaa-5cf2-a342-0dd3523e12a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397214} {:answer/id #uuid "bc315ebe-9716-5917-b194-2a4a36b373d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397215} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce119a8-474d-516f-b714-f6645bb4943e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397213} :answer-field/id #uuid "6451528f-6193-5fce-847d-24fbb635dd58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f942b92-7058-551e-856d-94bbeff34573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397218} {:answer/id #uuid "e417753c-3dcf-5b7e-8c3e-55d245a9b3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397219} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff25083-915f-5352-b85b-65678f3833df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397220} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eb5523-b928-553e-8031-34e6f2d343f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397221} {:answer/id #uuid "182b3678-ac7b-5812-986d-8764905ed502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{14}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397222} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e0c9b5-cbc2-598f-97c0-6c5a9eeadf86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba070694-f520-5989-9c36-fcafe8653bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338837" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-14\\lambda {y}^{′}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-14\\lambda m+49{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-7\\lambda )(m-7\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=7\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{7\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{7\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{7\\lambda x}+7B\\lambda x{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2B{e}^{14\\lambda }+B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }+\\frac{14}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }(1+\\frac{14}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{14\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{14}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f13d3f5c-4669-55f0-8d8e-50bb1b38e0c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530560}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529571}]}
